#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8eb340e-8c49-5478-8cca-a340e9a266e3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles"}] :question/id #uuid "69ba4325-7189-590d-95dd-b11487b632c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-230" :question/problem "An $8\\,\\text{m}$ tall lamppost is casting a shadow of a $2\\,\\text{m}$ man onto the ground. The man walks away from the lamppost with a speed of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the man's shadow changing?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/29/29ef832326fff9e5b274c92ae51f2f32a88d463980fd4c109773086c344a76bb.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the man's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the man's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the man's velocity is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp, and therefore, the distance between him and the lamp *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{8} & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ 2x+2y & =8y \\\\ 2x & =6y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} =2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (2.4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.4}{3} \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the man's shadow increases at a rate of $0.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa831011-1184-5d09-b4e8-b1192443d1b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles and Negative Rates"}] :question/id #uuid "6818c8ab-41e3-546d-a427-304316324bf0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7829" :question/problem "A $6\\,\\text{m}$ tall street lamp is casting a shadow of a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ woman onto the ground. The woman is walking towards the lamppost with a speed of $1.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the woman's shadow decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0b/0bdd4280035b896f113e8d7a27599e488958dae40235faad1658e85710465211.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the woman's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the woman's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the woman's velocity is *negative* because she is walking *toward* the lamp, and therefore the distance between her and the lamp *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6} & =\\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\ 1.5x+1.5y & =6y \\\\ 1.5x & =4.5y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac13 \\cdot (-1.5) = -0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the woman's shadow is decreasing at $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8eb340e-8c49-5478-8cca-a340e9a266e3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0757798a-d35e-53fe-88b3-ace0b231d134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520775} {:answer/id #uuid "0870faae-d3eb-5155-8021-e531dbbd3c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.75\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520777} {:answer/id #uuid "57ad84bd-7f76-5f14-a371-5fcb1e34b4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520961} {:answer/id #uuid "86ac4c6a-129c-59b0-85ac-975897dfd995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521071} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f3f234-42e4-5382-ac86-4b1e4fa9e6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0870faae-d3eb-5155-8021-e531dbbd3c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.75\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520777} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac48d99-1070-55c4-a6a8-9b39da55141b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb404ed-6af7-5b0d-8507-6193eb5d4f21" :question/math-academy-id "q-115973" :question/problem "A child on a scooter is running away from a $345\\,\\text{cm}$ tall lamppost at $4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ The height of the child on the scooter is $96.6\\,cm.$\nHow fast is the length of the child's shadow changing?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/fe/fefe072c51da577b504d81ccd1bb3546e77bca9bf9885950ca0c0c2091f9d335.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the child's distance from the lamppost, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the child's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the child's velocity is *positive* because he is running *away* from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the child and the lamppost *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{345} & =\\frac{y}{96.6} \\\\ 96.6x+96.6y & =345y \\\\ 96.6x & =248.4y \\\\ y & =\\frac{966}{2484}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{18}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{7}{18}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot (4.5) \\\\  & =1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the child's shadow increases at approximately $1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8eb340e-8c49-5478-8cca-a340e9a266e3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed3bf0f-a01b-541c-b8ee-63a118a81984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520861} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd2dc90-cad4-5638-8a72-65fe17c4461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520900} {:answer/id #uuid "79e79f28-e54f-5568-95d8-b58e116ac263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521037} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b36bf1-06bf-576b-bb3f-12c4605e9e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521241} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d3a69-30b3-5410-b26b-3b296c69af9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4b36bf1-06bf-576b-bb3f-12c4605e9e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521241} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7359e1c-94ec-5bfa-8fe5-9267e633fd0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28170d6f-d1bf-54a6-9dab-69ada5f8b07e" :question/math-academy-id "q-118674" :question/problem "A bus is traveling at night at $50\\,\\text{km/h}.$  After running past a $6.25\\,\\text{m}$ tall lamppost, the bus driver notices that the shadow of the bus is growing. \nIf the height of the bus is $3.75\\,m,$ how fast is the length of the shadow growing while it travels away from the lamp?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data. \n\n![](images/10/1094154aa39b3d9f1206d23ca920d5717a7f344c4a9486e9c4604752cf0ce623.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the bus' distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the runner's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{km/h}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the bus's velocity is *positive* because it is moving *away* from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the bus and the lamp *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6.25} & =\\frac{y}{3.75} \\\\ 3.75x+3.75y & =6.25y \\\\ 3.75x & =2.5y \\\\ y & =1.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =1.5\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{km/h}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =75\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the bus' shadow increases at $75\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa831011-1184-5d09-b4e8-b1192443d1b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles and Negative Rates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11afe4da-eb76-532c-a5bf-992c42de281f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.7\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520801} {:answer/id #uuid "3151adac-c8e6-5f77-89a0-dd3d2a1beeba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520868} {:answer/id #uuid "65e05d29-0bf5-5ebe-b6df-85f73f9e8f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520995} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7a9d7a-9961-5fa1-8ab5-6bd292fc7647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521011} {:answer/id #uuid "b89a01a1-4797-5637-a4bc-f3f9761f223c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b89a01a1-4797-5637-a4bc-f3f9761f223c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521174} :answer-field/id #uuid "7519ca12-0941-5f99-b046-39f8b4d54045" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02ba8f64-b770-56fd-871d-5b790d9828ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-121321" :question/problem "A man on a boat is sailing towards a $247\\,\\text{ft}$ high lighthouse. If the height of the man above the sea is $10\\,\\text{ft},$ how fast is the man approaching the lighthouse if his shadow is shrinking at a rate of $0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/6c/6c85e0c34b34dd9dcb384bb601b9b87a1a6b7174888c9ad49d8c2cd37abc13de.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance from the man to the lighthouse, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the man's shadow's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the man's shadow's velocity is *negative* because he is walking *toward* the lighthouse, and therefore the distance between him and the lighthouse *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{247} & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ 10x+10y & =247y \\\\ 10x & =237y \\\\ x & =23.7y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(23.7y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =23.7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =23.7(-0.25)≈-5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the man's speed is approximately $5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8eb340e-8c49-5478-8cca-a340e9a266e3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2681d8a2-6bbd-5d1d-af53-63f3d9de1132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520848} {:answer/id #uuid "814887dc-bf0f-5579-9b42-073ee2d25f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521061} {:answer/id #uuid "9378057a-2722-5968-9412-eb4384b8c99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521100} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e2a3a-3385-517d-aab2-26922f27d1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521221} {:answer/id #uuid "fecd5d50-1fec-5772-8841-508e263e498f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fecd5d50-1fec-5772-8841-508e263e498f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521323} :answer-field/id #uuid "d850485a-6c7b-52e0-a49c-5682fa3a56a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47dbce9b-0a44-511c-bf05-d91c2a66fd05" :question/math-academy-id "q-30324" :question/problem "A $60\\,\\text{in}$ tall runner is training at night in a park. While running past a lamppost that is $100\\,\\text{in}$ tall, she notices her shadow is growing. Her watch tells her that she is travelling at $50\\,\\text{in/s}.$ How fast is the length of her shadow changing while she runs away from the lamppost?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/4e/4ef1ba4975846a5375a18dee6cb4a55ba84d22d7c98b19eeaeebbb6b0017b623.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the runner's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the person's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the runner's velocity is positive because she is running away from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the runner and the lamp increases with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{100} & =\\frac{y}{60} \\\\ 60x+60y & =100y \\\\ 60x & =40y \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (50) \\\\  & =\\frac{150}{2} \\\\  & =75\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the runner's shadow increases at $75\\,\\text{in/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa831011-1184-5d09-b4e8-b1192443d1b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles and Negative Rates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d53056-7e04-552a-97a2-f96f42bb83e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520929} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3e823b-91e3-51e8-a0cb-926a35e57614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521039} {:answer/id #uuid "bb75f8de-5e82-53f3-8bbc-2a15c7126525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521184} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c78d28-2aa8-591d-95ff-19750406894f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521278} {:answer/id #uuid "f7997f4a-8d5d-54c8-9130-3c024f1af16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7997f4a-8d5d-54c8-9130-3c024f1af16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521307} :answer-field/id #uuid "240120c2-1821-54c3-9f09-fa9019bdaf10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21055998-26d2-5a03-b3c0-e38582a8d839" :question/math-academy-id "q-30330" :question/problem "Harry shines a flashlight on a $10\\,\\text{in}$ tall cat approaching him. If he is holding the flashlight $30\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and the animal's shadow is changing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{in/s},$ how fast is the cat approaching Harry?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/02/021a84ea0ad36014569028c0ffb39ab9b30f9225cf4bfcccba2d60050754a6f9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between Harry and the cat, while $y$ is the length of the cat's shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the velocity of the cat's shadow\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the velocity of the cat's shadow is *negative* because the cat is walking *toward* Harry, and therefore the distance between Harry and the cat *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{30} & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ 10x+10y & =30y \\\\ 10x & =20y \\\\ x & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2(-11)=-22\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the cat is $22\\,\\text{in/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa831011-1184-5d09-b4e8-b1192443d1b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles and Negative Rates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b772459-363c-5a44-a11a-d79c4d0978f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b772459-363c-5a44-a11a-d79c4d0978f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520856} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afa75a5-ca2e-53b7-86f8-9f195e705814" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53b5eaef-0c6b-5313-b037-03a8dbcbc92d" :question/math-academy-id "q-313985" :question/problem "A spotlight $S$ is on the ground $6\\,\\text{m}$ away from a wall and a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall robot is moving towards the wall at a rate of $0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the robot is $2.5\\,\\text{m}$ from the spotlight?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/e9/e91c4e08aa785de1fe500c2f43e0981501ee597bb7bb042827154842ec06ad0c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the robot’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the robot’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the robot’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(6-x)+x} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{9}{6-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{(6-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=6-2.5=3.5\\,\\text{m}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{{2.5}^{2}}\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8eb340e-8c49-5478-8cca-a340e9a266e3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e467d058-53f5-502f-93d5-68a99ee8bddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e467d058-53f5-502f-93d5-68a99ee8bddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521277} :answer-field/id #uuid "a51abb1a-dee0-5315-ba3a-f76763530a44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f7c2967-af65-5004-9025-62af739a76dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-344428" :question/problem "A $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall student is jogging away from a $4.0\\,\\text{m}$ parking lot light at $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the length of the student’s shadow changing?\n\nThe length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/e5/e500e84e039b0f8bef6afc7e636d6142309d65f13017c1f817fdf159b3f4d77c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the student’s distance from the parking lot light, while $y$ is the length of their shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the student’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the student’s velocity is *positive* because they are jogging *away* from the parking lot light, and therefore, the distance between the student and the light *increases* with time. \n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4.0} & =\\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\ 1.5x+1.5y & =4.0y \\\\ 1.5x & =2.5y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1.5}{2.5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{25}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot (2.4) \\\\  & ≈1.4\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately $1.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8eb340e-8c49-5478-8cca-a340e9a266e3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2484da21-5755-5bab-8027-716d1b529937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2484da21-5755-5bab-8027-716d1b529937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520839} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c7671a-533d-5202-b5e2-063b234bf23e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f5cbdde-4826-5bb5-88c4-7a602876e76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-344484" :question/problem "The members of a robotics club are testing a small robot in a courtyard lit by only one large lamp post.  Sam, a $1.4\\,\\text{m}$ student, is walking away from the post, and his shadow is increasing at a rate of $0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ If the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than  Sam, how fast is  Sam walking away from the post?\n\nSam's velocity is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than Sam, the height of the post is $7+1.4=8.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nLet's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/1c/1c17902179842d470f95519599235a9998e4cfbe538a44c201e34c3627c493af.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is Sam's distance from the lamp post, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the length of Sam's shadow changes at the rate\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the rate of change of the length of Sam's shadow is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp post, and therefore, the length of the shadow *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{8.4} & =\\frac{y}{1.4} \\\\ 1.4x+1.4y & =8.4y \\\\ 1.4x & =7y \\\\ x & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5y) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\cdot (0.8) \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sam's velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa831011-1184-5d09-b4e8-b1192443d1b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles and Negative Rates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7218284d-fc0a-5fae-8251-9a209394cdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7218284d-fc0a-5fae-8251-9a209394cdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521019} :answer-field/id #uuid "d49bd1c4-d01f-5541-8a31-35843fff408d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98780b5d-6c50-5c2a-867a-a1e2a96de8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344494" :question/problem "A bright lantern $L$ is on the ground $18\\,\\text{ft}$ away from a wall and a $6\\,\\text{ft}$ tall actor is moving towards the wall at a rate of $3\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the actor is $8\\,\\text{ft}$ from the lantern?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0a/0a47e801db651c027415a7943e5327bd378c1a08e533dc69673ab4304c8cc7b4.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the actor’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the actor’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the actor’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(18-x)+x} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{18} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{108}{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{108}{18-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{(18-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=18-8=10\\,\\text{ft}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa831011-1184-5d09-b4e8-b1192443d1b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles and Negative Rates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57960f1a-d541-53c1-b51f-0014873ffbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57960f1a-d541-53c1-b51f-0014873ffbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520960} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4acc90d-32b0-5012-bd13-3bc586d538d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91d63402-0442-54f2-9dc3-353df5f34caa" :question/math-academy-id "q-344508" :question/problem "Ethan is walking toward a $360\\,\\text{in}$ light pole, which has a lamp on top. If Ethan's height is $72\\,\\text{in}$ and his shadow is changing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{in/s},$ how fast is he walking toward the light pole?\n\nEthan is moving at a speed of \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/4e/4e82a1365099bcf76ae91b75d0d1ad276e4c35f8b172fc60b4d22e9156128d7e.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance from Ethan to the light pole, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that Ethan's shadow's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that Ethan's shadow's velocity is *negative* because he is walking *toward* the light pole, and therefore the distance between him and the light pole *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{360} & =\\frac{y}{72} \\\\ 72x+72y & =360y \\\\ 72x & =288y \\\\ x & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4(-6)=-24\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ethan's speed is  $24\\,\\text{in/s}.$"}]}
